WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:05.710
Terminator: Dark Fate has arrived, and it's
safe to say that fans have a lot of questions.

00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:09.330
Which works out really well, because we happen
to have a lot of answers!

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:11.940
Answers that require spoilers, of course.

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:16.680
With that warning, let's break down the ending
of Terminator: Dark Fate.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:21.619
The first two thirds of Terminator: Dark Fate
set the table: a new evil Terminator model

00:00:21.619 --> 00:00:26.589
called the Rev-9 has been sent from the future
to kill a Mexican woman named Daniella.

00:00:26.589 --> 00:00:31.890
Luckily, someone also sent back a bionic woman
from the future named Grace to protect her.

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:36.649
Along with Sarah Connor, looking to get revenge
after her son John was killed by a Terminator,

00:00:36.649 --> 00:00:42.059
they end up finding help from a very unlikely
source: Arnold Schwarzenegger's T-800, who

00:00:42.059 --> 00:00:46.480
has been stuck in the past since killing John
after the events of Terminator 2.

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:51.420
The real surprise here, though, is that this
Terminator has voluntarily given up the terminating

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:55.649
business and has settled down in a human life
under the name Carl.

00:00:55.649 --> 00:01:00.649
He embodies the core message from T2 that
people can choose their own fate and rewrite

00:01:00.649 --> 00:01:01.859
their future.

00:01:01.859 --> 00:01:07.120
He decided to stop being an evil killing machine,
and instead become a nurturing father figure.

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:11.690
It's an unexpected twist that suggests humans
and robots may actually be able to live together

00:01:11.690 --> 00:01:16.790
in peace after all if the robots simply choose
a different path.

00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:21.590
One of the big mysteries in Terminator: Dark
Fate is just why the robots have sent their

00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:23.850
agent back in time to kill Dani.

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:28.660
During the final chase scene, though, Grace
reveals the truth: that Dani herself actually

00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:30.870
becomes the resistance leader in the future.

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:36.880
As Sarah puts it, she's the new John Connor
the new last, best hope for all mankind.

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:42.700
Like Carl, Dani's character arc also reinforces
the idea that you can choose your own fate.

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:46.960
Several times in the film, the others urge
her to run and hide, but she instead insists

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:48.610
on fighting back.

00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:53.650
She chooses to become the hero humanity needs,
and indeed, it's those choices that actually

00:01:53.650 --> 00:01:55.290
make her a hero.

00:01:55.290 --> 00:02:00.290
It's a real contrast to John, who often seemed
to reject the role of savior, only reluctantly

00:02:00.290 --> 00:02:02.500
being pressed into it by circumstance.

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:07.280
John as a character seemed consigned by fate,
while Dani chooses her own path.

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:10.970
"I want to stand, and fight!"

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:15.930
The last third of Terminator: Dark Fate is
essentially one giant, neverending action

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:21.400
sequence that involves car chases, helicopters,
multiple plane crashes, gun fights, and a

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:25.410
Humvee getting swept over a dam, leading to
an underwater brawl.

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:29.660
It's such a whirlwind of non-stop excitement
that you may not have noticed it's all very,

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:31.290
very familiar.

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:35.260
Basically every action beat in Terminator:
Dark Fate is based off of something from a

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:37.790
previous Terminator installment.

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:40.510
In a way, that's thematically appropriate.

00:02:40.510 --> 00:02:46.070
Even though Sarah successfully destroyed Skynet
in T2, which should have led to a happy future,

00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:50.870
we learn instead that Skynet was just replaced
by a new AI called Legion.

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:55.210
The details of the future are different, but
the outline is still the same, just as the

00:02:55.210 --> 00:02:59.350
details of Dark Fate are different from the
previous films, but the plot is still the

00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:00.350
same.

00:03:00.350 --> 00:03:06.100
The message now seems to be the opposite of
T2: in Dark Fate, no matter what you do, nothing

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:08.930
ever really changes your destiny.

00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:13.920
In the climax of the film, the whole team
of good guys faces off against the Rev-9,

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:19.290
which seems unfair until you consider the
Rev-9 has the ability to split into two different,

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:24.830
self-sufficient robots - a metal exoskeleton
like the T-800, and a fluid form like the

00:03:24.830 --> 00:03:25.830
T-1000.

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:30.590
The only way the good guys are able to win
is by Grace sacrificing herself so Dani can

00:03:30.590 --> 00:03:36.270
take Grace's power source and jam it into
Rev-9's eyehole, causing it to melt down.

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:41.310
As it happens, fans have seen this before,
because that's almost exactly how Arnold's

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:46.709
good guy Terminator defeated the evil T-X
in Terminator 3: he removes his power core

00:03:46.709 --> 00:03:52.050
and shoves it in the T-X's mouth, whereupon
it explodes and destroys both of them.

00:03:52.050 --> 00:03:56.540
We're not sure if this is a loving homage
to Terminator 3, or if they just assumed everyone

00:03:56.540 --> 00:03:59.319
had forgotten Terminator 3 existed at all.

00:03:59.319 --> 00:04:00.480
Either could be true!

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:05.180
With the power core delivered to the Rev-9's
head, there's just one last thing that needs

00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:10.750
to happen: a sad descent into the pit where
both the villainous Rev-9 and the heroic T-800

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:14.380
model known as Carl blink their final blinky
lights.

00:04:14.380 --> 00:04:19.090
It's sad or at least it's supposed to be sad,
because it's very intentionally calling back

00:04:19.090 --> 00:04:21.389
memories of the infamous ending of T-2.

00:04:21.389 --> 00:04:23.750
You remember that one, right?

00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:25.539
It's the one with the thumbs up!

00:04:25.539 --> 00:04:30.150
It's obvious director Tim Miller is hoping
the memory of T2's tearjerker ending will

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:34.409
add some much needed gravitas to the climax
of Terminator: Dark Fate.

00:04:34.409 --> 00:04:38.539
Avengers: Endgame isn't the only action movie
this year that gets its emotional resonance

00:04:38.539 --> 00:04:40.419
from the films that came before.

00:04:40.419 --> 00:04:43.520
"I love you 3000!"

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:48.069
That's not the final "moving" moment designed
to play on peoples' emotions, though.

00:04:48.069 --> 00:04:52.610
The final scene of Terminator: Dark Fate is
designed to deliver a double subliminal whammy

00:04:52.610 --> 00:04:55.620
to anyone who has seen Terminator and T2.

00:04:55.620 --> 00:05:00.680
Dani and Sarah Connor are at a playground,
where they watch a young and oblivious Grace

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:02.510
playing with her family.

00:05:02.510 --> 00:05:06.580
After Dani vows to make sure Grace doesn't
have to sacrifice herself, she and Sarah jump

00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:10.979
in a waiting vehicle and drive off to prepare
for the impending Armageddon.

00:05:10.979 --> 00:05:15.089
The playground element, of course, is a clear
reference to the infamous playground scene

00:05:15.089 --> 00:05:21.009
in T2 where Sarah connnor watches families
get incinerated by the Skynet-caused nuclear

00:05:21.009 --> 00:05:22.009
holocaust.

00:05:22.009 --> 00:05:27.240
And the "riding into the sunset" ending is
exactly the same as the end of the first Terminator

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:32.009
film only this time, instead of being pregnant
with the future savior of humanity that she

00:05:32.009 --> 00:05:36.080
has to train, Sarah Connor is riding shotgun
with her.

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:41.650
Structurally, Terminator: Dark Fate is almost
identical to the original Terminator film,

00:05:41.650 --> 00:05:47.379
in much the same way that Star Wars: Episode
VII The Force Awakens is a soft reboot of

00:05:47.379 --> 00:05:50.710
the original Star Wars: Episode IV A New Hope.

00:05:50.710 --> 00:05:56.009
So we actually have a pretty good idea where
they could go with the next film in the series

00:05:56.009 --> 00:05:57.850
a new version of T2!

00:05:57.850 --> 00:06:02.629
Sure, the plot would actually have to be pretty
different, but the main thrust of T2 was the

00:06:02.629 --> 00:06:05.990
attempt to prevent Armageddon by destroying
Skynet.

00:06:05.990 --> 00:06:10.169
In Dark Fate, they don't ever have the chance
to even think about stopping the apocalypse,

00:06:10.169 --> 00:06:15.150
but they could certainly try to take steps
in Terminator 7 to prevent the new AI, Legion,

00:06:15.150 --> 00:06:16.719
from coming to be.

00:06:16.719 --> 00:06:21.129
One thing is for sure: we can't wait to find
out which beloved scenes from the previous

00:06:21.129 --> 00:06:24.180
Terminator films they'll remake in the next
one!

